<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP is permitted, on purpose.

  This app talks to a backend that the person reviewing it runs on their own machine — on an
  emulator (10.0.2.2), over a USB tunnel (localhost via `adb reverse`), or across a local network
  at whatever address that machine happens to have. There is no hosted endpoint, so there is no
  HTTPS to insist on.

  The obvious tighter version — permit cleartext only for the local addresses — is not expressible.
  Android's network security config matches domain names, not address ranges, so 10.0.2.2 and
  localhost can be listed but "any private address on this network" cannot. A reviewer typing their
  PC's LAN address into Settings is the main way this app is used, so the permission has to be broad
  enough to cover it.

  What this is not: user-installed certificate authorities are still not trusted here (only
  src="system"), so this build cannot be transparently intercepted by a proxy the way the debug
  variant can.

  If this app is ever pointed at a hosted backend, this file is the one line to change:
  set cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" and set android:usesCleartextTraffic="false" in
  AndroidManifest.xml, and the build becomes HTTPS-only again. See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
